1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,727
Thirty seconds from now, you're going
to see a spacecraft do something

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:06,095
no orbital Starship has ever done.

3
00:00:06,478 --> 00:00:10,577
It falls through Earth's atmosphere,
slows down, flips upright,

4
00:00:10,751 --> 00:00:12,597
and aims for a launch tower.

5
00:00:12,806 --> 00:00:15,081
No runway. No landing legs.

6
00:00:15,406 --> 00:00:19,098
Just two enormous steel
arms, waiting to grab it.

7
00:00:20,602 --> 00:00:23,017
On November fourteenth,
twenty twenty-six,

8
00:00:23,168 --> 00:00:27,162
SpaceX caught the Starship spacecraft
after its trip around Earth.

9
00:00:27,672 --> 00:00:29,692
But there's a twist
in this success story.

10
00:00:29,936 --> 00:00:32,560
The rocket's giant booster
never made it back.

11
00:00:33,071 --> 00:00:36,032
And that contrast tells us
far more about the future

12
00:00:36,066 --> 00:00:39,399
of reusable rockets
than any celebration clip.

13
00:00:41,513 --> 00:00:44,636
Let's rewind to Starbase,
Texas, one day earlier.

14
00:00:45,077 --> 00:00:48,108
Flight fifteen lifted off
on November thirteenth.

15
00:00:51,633 --> 00:00:54,408
At the bottom
was the Super Heavy booster, built

16
00:00:54,466 --> 00:00:58,042
to do the brutally hard work
of lifting everything off the ground.

17
00:00:58,367 --> 00:01:00,364
On top was Ship forty-two,

18
00:01:00,608 --> 00:01:04,311
the spacecraft built to keep flying
after the two stages separate.

19
00:01:04,636 --> 00:01:06,285
And the plan was bold:

20
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:09,559
bring both of them back
to the tower on the same flight.

21
00:01:11,272 --> 00:01:13,756
A few minutes into the flight,
the stages parted.

22
00:01:15,539 --> 00:01:19,754
And from that moment, this mission
became two very different stories.

23
00:01:20,566 --> 00:01:22,830
Super Heavy turned back
toward the coast.

24
00:01:23,074 --> 00:01:26,801
Ship forty-two kept accelerating,
heading for orbit.

25
00:01:28,404 --> 00:01:31,586
The booster was supposed
to return to the launch tower too.

26
00:01:32,189 --> 00:01:35,800
Super Heavy boosters had been caught
by the tower on earlier flights,

27
00:01:35,870 --> 00:01:37,669
so this part was not new.

28
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:40,932
But during its return,
the catch was called off.

29
00:01:41,315 --> 00:01:44,287
Its systems ruled out
a safe approach to the tower,

30
00:01:44,438 --> 00:01:48,026
and the booster was diverted
toward the Gulf of Mexico instead.

31
00:01:48,467 --> 00:01:49,918
It did not come back.

32
00:01:50,359 --> 00:01:53,877
Early reports differ on exactly
what triggered the abort,

33
00:01:54,028 --> 00:01:56,513
and the root cause
has not been confirmed.

34
00:01:56,838 --> 00:02:01,087
That matters: we know what
was reported, not the final reason.

35
00:02:02,528 --> 00:02:04,943
So one half of the rocket was lost.

36
00:02:05,384 --> 00:02:07,079
Yet the other half
was just beginning

37
00:02:07,126 --> 00:02:09,355
the most ambitious
part of its journey.

38
00:02:11,359 --> 00:02:15,376
Ship forty-two reached orbit,
roughly two hundred seventy-five

39
00:02:15,422 --> 00:02:18,592
kilometers above Earth,
according to the mission plan.

40
00:02:18,975 --> 00:02:22,353
It deployed twenty-six
Starlink V3 satellites.

41
00:02:22,632 --> 00:02:25,709
Three of them were planned
to carry cameras to help inspect

42
00:02:25,743 --> 00:02:26,928
the ship's heat shield.

43
00:02:27,253 --> 00:02:30,643
Instead of rushing home,
Starship kept circling the planet.

44
00:02:30,852 --> 00:02:31,990
Fourteen orbits.

45
00:02:32,268 --> 00:02:34,671
More than twenty-two
hours in space.

46
00:02:34,671 --> 00:02:39,168
Now the big question was not
whether Starship could reach orbit.

47
00:02:39,447 --> 00:02:41,978
It was whether it could come
back from orbit and still

48
00:02:42,048 --> 00:02:44,497
be precise enough
to meet a steel tower.

49
00:02:44,938 --> 00:02:48,816
In low orbit, a spacecraft has to move
at roughly seventeen thousand

50
00:02:48,874 --> 00:02:52,891
miles per hour, about twenty-eight
thousand kilometers per hour,

51
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:54,761
just to keep from falling back.

52
00:02:55,144 --> 00:02:58,476
At that speed, you don't
simply drop straight down.

53
00:02:58,859 --> 00:03:02,736
You're moving sideways around the planet
so fast that you have to shed

54
00:03:02,783 --> 00:03:05,860
an enormous amount of energy
before you can come home.

55
00:03:07,463 --> 00:03:11,225
First came the deorbit burn,
a short engine firing that bends

56
00:03:11,283 --> 00:03:13,721
the ship's path down
into the atmosphere.

57
00:03:15,304 --> 00:03:17,382
Then the air
did most of the braking.

58
00:03:17,765 --> 00:03:19,937
Air piled up in front
of the vehicle.

59
00:03:20,111 --> 00:03:21,307
Temperatures soared.

60
00:03:21,690 --> 00:03:24,314
Glowing plasma wrapped
around the ship.

61
00:03:24,488 --> 00:03:27,716
Its heat shield tiles
had to protect the steel structure,

62
00:03:27,867 --> 00:03:29,956
while the flaps steered the fall.

63
00:03:31,481 --> 00:03:33,896
And this is where the
landing gets strange.

64
00:03:34,175 --> 00:03:35,986
An airplane needs a runway.

65
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:39,202
A returning capsule usually
drops under parachutes,

66
00:03:39,353 --> 00:03:40,723
often into the ocean.

67
00:03:41,048 --> 00:03:44,520
Starship fell belly-first,
steering with its flaps.

68
00:03:44,845 --> 00:03:47,236
Then it lit its engines
and swung upright

69
00:03:47,294 --> 00:03:49,082
for a final powered approach.

70
00:03:50,707 --> 00:03:53,041
The target was not
a big landing pad.

71
00:03:53,215 --> 00:03:56,431
It was the gap between the tower's
two mechanical arms,

72
00:03:56,547 --> 00:03:59,182
the ones SpaceX calls
the chopsticks.

73
00:03:59,507 --> 00:04:00,924
Why go to all this trouble?

74
00:04:01,365 --> 00:04:02,723
Landing legs are heavy,

75
00:04:02,851 --> 00:04:06,566
and every kilogram of legs
is a kilogram that can't be payload.

76
00:04:06,845 --> 00:04:11,628
The idea is to leave that heavy
hardware on the ground, in the tower,

77
00:04:11,698 --> 00:04:13,625
and let the rocket fly lighter.

78
00:04:15,229 --> 00:04:17,249
Think about how small
that margin is.

79
00:04:17,458 --> 00:04:20,314
The tower cannot chase
a rocket across Texas.

80
00:04:20,639 --> 00:04:23,774
Starship has to put itself
in exactly the right place,

81
00:04:23,925 --> 00:04:25,724
at exactly the right speed,

82
00:04:25,875 --> 00:04:29,312
while the arms wait for the very end
of the landing sequence.

83
00:04:29,753 --> 00:04:32,516
A successful catch
doesn't mean this is easy.

84
00:04:32,725 --> 00:04:35,221
It means the team
proved one crucial piece

85
00:04:35,268 --> 00:04:37,207
of the system in real flight.

86
00:04:38,848 --> 00:04:39,985
And then it happened.

87
00:04:40,589 --> 00:04:43,921
On November fourteenth,
about twenty-two and a half hours

88
00:04:43,979 --> 00:04:48,054
after liftoff, Ship forty-two
came back to Starbase.

89
00:04:48,658 --> 00:04:51,282
Its engines slowed
the final descent.

90
00:04:51,723 --> 00:04:55,577
It settled between the two arms,
and the tower caught it.

91
00:05:02,018 --> 00:05:06,407
For the first time, a Starship
upper stage had come home from orbit

92
00:05:06,616 --> 00:05:10,334
and been caught at its launch site.

93
00:05:10,334 --> 00:05:13,086
This is where a lot of headlines
get a little too excited.

94
00:05:13,469 --> 00:05:15,268
Catching a spacecraft
is not the same

95
00:05:15,315 --> 00:05:17,416
as proving it
can fly again tomorrow.

96
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:20,098
Engineers still need inspections.

97
00:05:20,307 --> 00:05:23,291
The heat shield, the engines,
the plumbing and the structure all

98
00:05:23,349 --> 00:05:26,159
have to survive
flight after flight.

99
00:05:26,762 --> 00:05:29,502
One catch does not prove
routine reuse.

100
00:05:29,746 --> 00:05:33,670
And remember: on this flight,
the booster was lost.

101
00:05:35,112 --> 00:05:36,470
So why does this matter?

102
00:05:36,853 --> 00:05:40,429
Because bringing an orbital
spacecraft back to its own launch site

103
00:05:40,638 --> 00:05:43,564
is a major step toward a rocket
you can fly again,

104
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:45,805
instead of building a new
one for every mission.

105
00:05:46,188 --> 00:05:47,906
That's the dream behind Starship:

106
00:05:48,185 --> 00:05:50,727
make reaching orbit
cheaper and more routine.

107
00:05:51,006 --> 00:05:52,817
But the dream isn't the data.

108
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:57,264
The data, as of today,
is one historic catch,

109
00:05:57,647 --> 00:06:01,641
twenty-six satellites deployed,
and one lost booster.

110
00:06:03,351 --> 00:06:06,045
What comes next
may matter even more.

111
00:06:06,556 --> 00:06:09,493
Can engineers inspect
this ship, fly it again,

112
00:06:09,516 --> 00:06:11,490
and repeat the process reliably?

113
00:06:11,931 --> 00:06:14,752
Can they recover both
stages on the same flight?

114
00:06:15,356 --> 00:06:17,248
Those are the milestones to watch.

115
00:06:18,959 --> 00:06:19,992
I'm Atlas.

116
00:06:20,376 --> 00:06:24,033
And the wildest part of this story
isn't that a rocket came home.

117
00:06:24,416 --> 00:06:26,947
It's that the next challenge
is making a moment like

118
00:06:26,982 --> 00:06:28,503
this look ordinary.
